{"t":1793267815,"office":"LWX","station":"KDCA","point":"38.8894,-77.0352","field":"38.9429,-76.5684","field_station":"KNAK","afd":{"id":"049a48ce-dfb8-447d-9d3a-ceb09ca1cabc","office":"LWX","issuanceTime":"2026-10-29T09:00:00+00:00","productText":"\n000\nFXUS61 KLWX 290900\nAFDLWX\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Baltimore MD/Washington DC\n500 AM EDT Thu Oct 29 2026\n\n.WHAT HAS CHANGED...\nHave issued a Dense Fog Advisory until 10 AM for portions of\nwestern Maryland east of the Allegheny Front, eastern West\nVirginia, and a small portion of the Shenandoah Valley in\nVirginia to cover where dense fog is most common, though patchy\ndense fog is possible bordering the advisory area at times.\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n- 1) Above normal temperatures through Friday, then seasonable \n  with increasing clouds this weekend. \n\n- 2) Rain chances remain uncertain for the first half of next \n  week.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Above normal temperatures through Friday, then \nseasonable with increasing clouds this weekend. \n\nA couple of consecutive days of light onshore flow has increased\nlow-level moisture enough to result in (1) river valley fog west\nof the Blue Ridge (locally dense at times), and (2) low clouds\nbanking along and east of the Blue Ridge and Catoctin Mountains.\nClouds/fog are more expansive relative to Wednesday morning, but\nshould burn off and lift by midday to early afternoon as winds\nshift from southeast to southwest.\n\nThe NAM seems to be handling the low-level moisture profiles\nbest, with low clouds favored east and areas of fog west (as\nopposed to a lot of other guidance which maintains a dense fog\nthreat to the east with minimal fog to the west).\n\nAfter the clouds break, mixing on these light southwest winds\nshould allow temperatures to reach into the lower to middle 70s\n(60s mountains). Low temperatures tonight will be seasonable\nin the 40s to lower 50s, but may be rather variable over short \ndistances depending on where goes calm and where holds onto a \nlight southwest wind. Higher elevations will actually hold on to\nmore southwest breezes and be poking up into the warming airmass,\nand could therefore be some of the warmest spots tonight.\n\nWell above normal temperatures persist Friday, despite a cold\nfront crossing the region. Cold air advection lags behind this\nfront, and downsloping flow likely allows for highs to push \nwell into the 70s (60s mountains). One caveat is if some \nstratocumulus develops on northwest flow, but the background \nairmass is rather dry so the chance of that happening and it \nhaving a significant impact on temperatures is limited.\n\nEventually, some cooler temperatures will filter into the region\nFriday night through the weekend. Expect seasonable and dry\nconditions as high pressure holds firm, with any precipitation\nbecoming less likely through at least Sunday evening.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Rain chances remain uncertain for the first \nhalf of next week. \n\nStrong high pressure will remain overhead Sunday and only gradually \ndrift eastward through the first half of next week. The Mid-Atlantic\nwill be caught in between more prominent but slow moving troughs\n(or closed lows) near Florida/the Bahamas, the Plains, and eastern\nCanada. Some models still show potential for a shortwave trough\nto ride around the departing ridge, but at this point the chance\nfor rain has dropped to 30 percent or less between Sunday night\nand Tuesday night. Even though conditions may largely remain dry,\nexpect more cloud cover between onshore surface flow and a warm\nadvection pattern aloft. Temperatures will likely remain a little\nabove normal through this period despite the extra clouds. The \nPlains trough may eventually increase rain chances Wednesday or \nThursday as it moves eastward, but its progression is uncertain \nas well.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION /09Z THURSDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nLow clouds (FL020-FL030) will continue to bank up the east side\nof the Blue Ridge and Catoctin Mountains, expanding across the\nI-95 corridor through daybreak. Ceilings may gradually lower,\npossibly breaching IFR 11Z-13Z, though given recent trends MVFR\nis more favored. Low clouds are beginning to show a slow \neastward shift due to south to southwest flow. To the west at\nMRB, fog is developing nearby, and may still become dense at \ntimes until about 13Z. Light SE flow early this morning becomes\nSW today through tonight with VFR expected to return 15Z-18Z. \nLow cloud/fog potential appears lower tonight given a persistent\nalbeit light SW wind.\n\nVFR is likely Friday as winds shift to the northwest. Gusts of\n20-25 knots are likely around midday into mid afternoon as a dry\ncold front crosses. Winds become lighter Friday night into\nSaturday while shifting to the north, with light east to calm\nwinds Saturday night as high pressure builds.\n\nSignificant weather is unlikely Sunday and Monday as high pressure \nmoves eastward. However, some low chances of sub-VFR ceilings and a \nfew rain showers exist Monday depending on how the weather pattern \nevolves.\n\n&&\n\n.MARINE...\nLight south to southwest winds are expected through tonight,\nthough a few gusts around 15 knots are possible at times. A dry\ncold front will shift winds to the northwest Friday, with near\nshore and narrower waterways having the potential to see gusts\nto near 20 knots at times. Northerly channeling Friday night may\nprompt Small Craft Advisories before diminishing Saturday. Winds\nthen shift to the east Saturday night as high pressure builds.\n\nLight winds will likely continue Sunday and Monday as high pressure \ngradually moves offshore.\n\n&&\n\n.TIDES/COASTAL FLOODING...\nTidal anomalies remain elevated, though have been slowly\ndecreasing so far tonight. Some fluctuation is possible over the\nnext 12-24 hours which could send vulnerable shoreline to near\nminor flood stage this evening.\n\nOffshore flow decreases tide levels Friday into Saturday. Winds\nturn back around out of the south at low speeds which could\ncause a modest rebound in tides by Sunday.\n\n&&\n\n.LWX WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nDC...None.\nMD...Dense Fog Advisory until 10 AM EDT this morning for MDZ501-502.\nVA...Dense Fog Advisory until 10 AM EDT this morning for VAZ027-029.\nWV...Dense Fog Advisory until 10 AM EDT this morning for WVZ050-055-\n     502-504.\nMARINE...None.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...ADS/DHOF/CPB/SRT\nAVIATION...ADS/DHOF/CPB/SRT\nMARINE...ADS/DHOF/CPB/SRT\nTIDES/COASTAL FLOODING...DHOF\n"},"forecast":{"t":1793267765,"days":{"2026-10-29":{"hi":72,"lo":52,"pop":1,"short":"Patchy Fog then Partly Sunny"},"2026-10-30":{"hi":75,"lo":46,"pop":1,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-10-31":{"hi":65,"lo":46,"pop":0,"short":"Partly Sunny"},"2026-11-01":{"hi":68,"lo":48,"pop":3,"short":"Partly Sunny"},"2026-11-02":{"hi":69,"lo":51,"pop":12,"short":"Mostly Cloudy"},"2026-11-03":{"hi":72,"lo":54,"pop":17,"short":"Partly Sunny"},"2026-11-04":{"hi":71,"lo":null,"pop":26,"short":"Chance Rain Showers"}}},"grid":{"t0":1793264400,"n":48,"ceil":[3400,10600,17800,25000,25000,25000,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null],"vis":[8.7,7.6,7.0,5.3,4.2,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0],"spd":[1,1,0,0,0,1,2,3,4,5,6,6,6,4,4,4,4,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,4,4,6,8,9,10,10,10,11,10,10,9,9,8,7,5,4,4,4,4,4,4],"gst":[3,3,2,3,3,3,4,5,6,7,7,8,8,8,8,9,9,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,9,9,9,9,11,12,13,14,14,15,15,15,14,13,13,11,11,10,10,10,11,11,11,11],"dir":[280,280,0,250,230,220,220,200,190,180,180,180,180,170,180,180,190,200,210,210,230,240,240,240,250,250,260,260,270,290,300,310,320,320,320,320,320,320,330,330,330,340,340,34